{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789588717,"nonce":"00TOq65rfFO3waSEFEOoZHzlcQQ=","title":"b4 trailers: get_body_parts can never return in-body git headers","message":"`LoreMessage.get_body_parts()` always returns an empty `githeaders` list, so\nin-body `From:`/`Subject:`/`Date:` headers are never recognised as headers.\nThey fall through into the commit message instead.\n\nReproducer\n----------\n\n```python\n\u003e\u003e\u003e b4.LoreMessage.get_body_parts(\n...     'From: Real Author \u003creal@example.com\u003e\\n\\nusb: fix the thing\\n')\ngitheaders: []\nmessage:    'From: Real Author \u003creal@example.com\u003e\\n\\n'\ntrailers:   [usb: fix the thing]\n```\n\nExpected: `githeaders` holds the `From:` line; `message` holds the subject.\n\nRoot cause\n----------\n\n`find_trailers()` (src/b4/__init__.py:2895) normalises its input with:\n\n```python\nbody = body.split('\\n-- \\n', 1)[0].strip() + '\\n'\n```\n\nand then iterates `body.split('\\n')`. Because a trailing newline was just\nappended, that split always yields one final empty string. The empty string\nmatches neither the trailer regex nor the extinfo branch, so it reaches the\nfallthrough at src/b4/__init__.py:2982 and is appended to `others`.\n\n`others` is therefore never empty, for any input whatsoever.\n\n`get_body_parts()` (src/b4/__init__.py:3075) decides whether the first\nparagraph is a header block by testing exactly that list:\n\n```python\nh, o = LoreMessage.find_trailers(bpara[0])\nif len(o):\n    # Not everything was a header, so we don't treat it as headers\n    mparts.append(bpara[0])\nelse:\n    githeaders = h\n```\n\n`len(o)` is always \u003e= 1, so the `else` branch is unreachable and `githeaders`\nstays at the `list()` it was initialised to on src/b4/__init__.py:3036.\n\nTwo further consequences follow from the same cause:\n\n- The `if githeaders == trailers:` guard at src/b4/__init__.py:3086, which\n  exists to catch a message consisting of nothing but trailers, is also\n  unreachable for a non-empty `trailers`.\n- The first paragraph is unconditionally pushed into `mparts`, so a message\n  whose body is only in-body headers has them re-read as trailers out of\n  `bpara[-1]`.\n\nConfirmed by execution against b4 at 538749e (v0.16.0-29-g538749e), not\nonly by reading.\n\nObservable symptom\n------------------\n\nMost callers discard `githeaders` or hand it straight back to\n`rebuild_message()`, where the headers round-trip through `message` largely\nintact, which is presumably why this has gone unnoticed.\n\nThe review TUI is the exception. src/b4/review_tui/_review_app.py:639 renders\nin-body git headers dimmed, to set them apart from the commit message:\n\n```python\nif bheaders:\n    for lt in bheaders:\n        _write_msg_line(Text(lt.as_string(), style='dim'))\n```\n\n`bheaders` is always empty, so a patch carrying an in-body `From:` shows that\nheader as ordinary, undimmed message text instead.\n\nIn-body `From:` is how a patch keeps correct authorship when it is relayed\nthrough a remailer or a list that rewrites the `From:` header, so this is the\ncase where telling the header apart from the message matters most.\n\nFound while porting this logic into liblore, where the empty `githeaders`\nmade in-body `From:` detection fail outright.","files":null}]}